From 673081ed7665c8d621ae4037002a0a20de0e78f5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim Besard <tim.besard@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 15:58:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] [SPIR-V] Lower llvm.minimum/maximum instead of selecting
 fmin/fmax (#225667)

Fixes #225665. The SPIRV-LLVM-Translator equivalent is
KhronosGroup/SPIRV-LLVM-Translator#4032.

`G_FMINIMUM`/`G_FMAXIMUM` were legal and selected to `fmin`/`fmax`
(`NMin`/`NMax`), which neither propagate NaNs nor order signed zeros.
This expands `llvm.minimum`/`llvm.maximum` in `SPIRVPrepareFunctions`,
with the same code the translator uses in `SPIRVRegularizeLLVM`, so that
both produce the same OpenCL SPIR-V:

```llvm
%m     = call float @llvm.minnum.f32(float %x, float %y)
; unless nsz: for non-NaN operands, the result has the sign of %x if that is
; set (maximum: clear), and the sign of %y otherwise
%xsign = call float @llvm.copysign.f32(float 1.0, float %x)
%xneg  = fcmp olt float %xsign, 0.0
%s     = select i1 %xneg, float %x, float %y
%m.s   = call float @llvm.copysign.f32(float %m, float %s)
; unless nnan
%uno   = fcmp uno float %x, %y
%r     = select i1 %uno, float 0x7FF8000000000000, float %m.s
```

The `copysign` on the result only changes a zero of the wrong sign. A
`nnan nsz` call still compiles to a single `fmin`/`fmax`.

`ExpandReductions` already turns `vector.reduce.fminimum`/`fmaximum` on
power-of-two vectors into `llvm.minimum`/`maximum` calls. The remaining
reductions are expanded into a chain of them, as the legalizer did
before. `G_FMINIMUM`/`G_FMAXIMUM` are no longer legal.

The expansion is done in IR because there the floating-point type is
exact. An earlier version of this PR used
`LegalizerHelper::lowerFMinimumMaximum`, and a later one a custom
legalization. Both had to give SPIR-V types to the registers they
create. The LLT does not distinguish `bfloat` from `half`, and the
registers created while widening vectors or expanding reductions have no
SPIR-V type to recover it from, so `bfloat` crashed in instruction
selection.

Two more changes were needed, each in its own commit with its own test:

- `fcmp ord`/`uno` in shaders were selected to
`OpOrdered`/`OpUnordered`. Those require the `Kernel` capability, so any
`fcmp ord`/`uno` in a Vulkan module was already invalid. In shaders they
are now selected via `OpIsNan`.
- With `SPV_EXT_long_vector`, `fcmp` and `copysign` on vectors whose
length isn't a power of two (e.g. `<5 x float>`) failed to legalize.
They are now widened like the other floating-point operations.

GLSL.std.450 has no `copysign`, so in shaders it is lowered to integer
operations, which makes `half`/`double` need `Int16`/`Int64`, the same
as `llvm.copysign` already does. `nsz` avoids this.

This supersedes the draft #180797, which used the generic lowering but
didn't handle the untyped `G_IS_FPCLASS` source (its CI fails on it) or
shaders, and had no tests. `minimumnum`/`maximumnum` are handled
separately in #218641.

Assisted by: Opus 5.5
